<<~ kanawai uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/ref/RFC-2119`>>



<<~ ahu slot=`#/head`>>

! Worker Message Protocol — the StructuredClone Boundary

The lararium runtime crosses one mandatory trust boundary: ''main thread ↔ Worker''. Every crossing uses `postMessage()`, which internally applies the ''structured clone algorithm'' (WHATWG HTML spec). The gap between "any JS object" and "a structured-clone-safe object" carries non-trivial constraints that shape the whole island message protocol.

This meme lifts that protocol out of the alignment-architecture doctrine so operator and agent review it together as implementation design.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/boundary`>>

!! What the Boundary Drops and Preserves

| Dropped by structured clone | Consequence |
|---|---|
| Prototype chain | class instances arrive as plain objects; the Worker engine cannot receive TW5 engine objects from main thread; class wrappers stay Worker-local |
| Property descriptors (getters/setters/readonly) | all fields arrive read/write plain; no defensive getter tricks survive |
| Functions | no callbacks cross; event handlers stay thread-local |
| Symbol-keyed fields | tiddler field names MUST NOT use Symbol keys (already true in the TW5 model) |
| Class private fields | internal state drops silently — no error, silent loss |
| DOM nodes | no widget or document references cross |

| Preserved | Use |
|---|---|
| plain objects `{ [key:string]: primitive }` | tiddler records — matches the TW5 field model |
| Map, Set, Array | changeset indexes, event queues |
| ArrayBuffer //(transferable)// | binary payloads — ''transfer'' moves them zero-copy and neuters the sender's buffer |
| TypedArray (Uint8Array) | content-addressed bytes (`cas:block`) — cloneable; buffer transferable |
| MessagePort //(transferable)// | the island's `syncPort` — transferred with the manifest, never cloned; CRDT sync rides it |
| CryptoKey | survives a clone, yet no island message carries one — key material stays in-thread (GP-4) |
| Error (name + message) | fault signals across the boundary |
| Date | timestamp fields |

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/envelope`>>

!! Golden Principles — the Message Envelope

The envelope's source of truth: `packages/lararium-mesh/src/island-protocol.ts`.

''GP-1 — A typed envelope.'' Every message MUST carry a `type` discriminant; its other fields ride flat beside it, each message type naming its own:

```json
{ "type": "<message-type>", "...": "the type's own fields" }
```
No naked payloads, and no version counter: a vessel and its islands run one build, so a frame shape changes once, everywhere, in one commit, and both type guards refuse a frame that names no type.

''GP-2 — Plain-object payloads only.'' No class instances, functions, or DOM. An `event` payload carries only `string | number | boolean` values; a relayed DOM event carries an explicit allowlist of `number | boolean` fields; relayed text rides its own message under one named field and one bound (`DOM_INPUT_MAX_CHARS`). All engine-side class wrappers stay Worker-local. One transferable rides the manifest: the `syncPort`.

''GP-3 — CRDT bytes ride the sync port, never an envelope.'' No message carries a changeset. The vessel transfers one `MessagePort` (`syncPort`) with the manifest — `postMessage(msg, [syncPort])`, transferred and never cloned — and the island boots its own Repo over it. CRDT sync through that port stands as the island's sole source of tiddler truth; the vessel delivers no tiddler deltas.

''GP-4 — Key material stays in-thread.'' No `CryptoKey` and no raw key bytes appear on this protocol surface. A proof or a verdict crosses (`daemon:verify-request`, `daemon:verify-result`); a key does not.

''GP-5 — Teardown handshake.'' On eviction the vessel sends `{ type: "teardown" }`. The island completes in-flight reactions, cancels all live handles, and replies `{ type: "teardown:ack" }` once each Repo it holds has shut down and closed its storage. The vessel then terminates the worker. The vessel MUST also close its end of the sync channel (`mainPort.close()`) in the teardown path; a port left open leaks the network adapter silently.

''GP-6 — No direct Worker-to-Worker messages.'' Islands never message each other. An island emits an `event` to the vessel, which routes it; shared state crosses between islands as CRDT sync through each island's own port.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/prior-art`>>

!! Prior Art

* ''Comlink'' (Google) — type-safe RPC over `postMessage`: Proxy wrapping + explicit transfer lists + schema-versioned call objects. The envelope convention.
* ''Redux DevTools bridge'' — actions MUST be plain-object serializable: if `JSON.stringify(action)` throws, it does not cross. The same constraint as tiddler fields.
* ''Electron IPC'' (`ipcMain`/`ipcRenderer`) — version the message shape; never send class instances; a `type` discriminant on every message. The direct antecedent of GP-1.
* ''React Native bridge → JSI'' — the old bridge required JSON-serializable payloads; the New Architecture (JSI) replaced it with direct calls. The Lares analogue of JSI: the in-Worker synchronous TW5 read (no boundary at all; engine + TW5 co-located).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/co-location`>>

!! The Co-Location Insight

TW5 and the reaction engine occupy the ''same'' Worker thread (co-located, synchronous reads) precisely to keep the StructuredClone boundary off the hot path. The engine reads tiddlers via `filterTiddlers()` — a synchronous in-memory call, no `postMessage`, no clone. Tiddler state never crosses as a message at all: it arrives by CRDT sync over the island's port (GP-3).

!!! The message set — derived, never listed from memory

`packages/lararium-mesh/src/island-protocol.ts` holds the whole set in two unions, `VesselToIslandMsg` and `IslandToVesselMsg`, and its two guards (`isVesselToIslandMsg`, `isIslandToVesselMsg`) name every `type` string a door accepts. The lists below copy those guards; where they differ from the source, the source rules.

''Vessel → island''
* ''Lifecycle'': `manifest` (carries the transferred `syncPort`, the recipe, the grants and the core hash) · `hooanu` · `teardown`.
* ''Daemon island'': `daemon:place-verb` · `telemetry:place-verb` · `structurepalace:kapae` · `daemon:derive-skeleton-request` · `daemon:worldline-compare-request` · `daemon:worldline-trajectory-request` · `daemon:verb-result` · `daemon:verify-request` · `daemon:resolve-binding-request` · `daemon:evict-result` · `daemon:residency-op-result`.
* ''Wiki island'': `wiki:place-verb` · `lar:control-event` · `lar:resend`.
* ''Sensorium'': an open namespace, `sensorium:<cap-verb>` (lower-kebab). The wire admits the namespace and each cap owns its own verbs, so a new sensory act edits no central list.
* ''Content'': `cas:block`.

''Island → vessel''
* ''Lifecycle'': `ready` · `ea` · `breath` · `fault` · `teardown:ack`.
* ''Events'': `event`, discriminated by `listenable`. A sensorium answer rides here. The camera's keyed frame never does: it rides `lar:frame` under its own name.
* ''Camera'': `lar:frame` · `lar:control-result` · `lar:service`.
* ''Wiki island'': `wiki:verb-result`.
* ''Daemon island'': `daemon:delegate-verb` · `daemon:derive-skeleton-result` · `daemon:worldline-compare-result` · `daemon:worldline-trajectory-result` · `daemon:verify-result` · `daemon:resolve-binding-result` · `daemon:evict-request` · `daemon:residency-op` · `daemon:wiki-alert`.
* ''Content'': `cas:want`.

!!! The camera's two legs

A browser camera renders inside the island and crosses the boundary twice, under the kinds of `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/lar-camera`. ''Out'': `lar:frame`, the keyed absolute frame of one camera on one surface under one mount epoch, each key whole under its own monotone revision, coalesced newest-wins; `lar:control-result` answers each act `applied`, `stale-target` or `refused` with a named reason; `lar:service` asks the surface to open a host door the manifest names. ''Back'': `lar:control-event` carries one act, sense, write or service answer, addressed to one control by exactly one of a control id, a render id or an affordance id, with the frame revision seen and a WHOLE value bounded at the island's door, never a diff and never a keycode; `lar:resend` asks a whole repaint under a new mount epoch. Each kind reads whole at its door through the contract's own reader, and a malformed one vanishes there as any malformed message does. The pool places a person's act with `placeControlEvent` and asks a whole repaint with `resendProjection`, both fire-and-forget and both dropped by a cooled slot. The island's frames, answers and host-door asks reach the vessel's handler the way an `event` does: an island outside any session reaches `onWorkerEvent`, a session's island reaches its own surface door alone.

!!! The thermal signals

* `hooanu` (hoʻoanu, "to cool") — cool an island from ''`wela`'' (live, reacting) to ''`anu`'' (cold); the thread terminates. Live handles cancel before a cool.
* No heating message exists. `anu` → `wela` (hoʻowela, "to heat") happens at the vessel: the pool spawns a fresh worker, which answers `ready`, takes its `manifest`, and declares `ea`.
* The thermal axis runs ''two-state'' (`wela` / `anu`) with an orthogonal pin-flag (pinned = exempt from cooling). No warm middle tier exists — see `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/residency-tiers#/third-tier-gate`.

<<~/ahu>>

